content.text:
The population grid exists in two layers: **source data** and **where you see it in-game**.

### Canonical data (edit here)

**`source/scenes/election_runtime.scene.dry`** — `DISTRICT_POPULATIONS` (lines ~82–106): district × stratum counts in **units of 10,000 people**.

```82:106:source/scenes/election_runtime.scene.dry
/** Population in 10,000s per district × stratum (sync with election-world-data.js). */
const DISTRICT_POPULATIONS = {
    Docks: {
        rich: 17, first: 42, second: 30, coop: 1, drone: 11, alien: 1, deeper: 1,
        third_wave: 1,
    },
    Aurora: {
        rich: 0, first: 58, second: 57, coop: 1, drone: 2, alien: 9, deeper: 3,
    },
    // ... Vats, Railyard, Pitts, Limelight, Deeps
}
```

That table is copied into **`source/lib/election-world-data.js`** for Node tools (`simulate-election`, validation). Keep both in sync (the JS file says so explicitly).

At runtime, `buildDistricts()` turns those vectors into `VoterBlock` population (`election_runtime.scene.dry` ~551–565). Home seeding reads `block.population` from those blocks.

### In-game HTML table

**`source/scenes/election_simulation.scene.dry`** — `@show_results`, section **“Populations (in 10,000s):”** (~30–49): rows = strata (Rich, First, Second, Clans, …), columns = districts, values like `[+ Aurora_coop_pop +]`.

Those qualities are filled by **`qified_pops()`** in `election_runtime.scene.dry` (~1039), which runs when projections update.

### Text reports (CLI / debug)

**`source/lib/election-v2.js`** — `formatPopulationTable()` used in `formatElectionReportV2()` (“Populations (in 10,000s):” in election sim output).

---

**Coop note:** `coop: 1` in almost every district is why Jacobs shows **1** coop home citywide — the table is the authority, not the report. To change coop housing, bump `coop` in `DISTRICT_POPULATIONS` (and the mirror in `election-world-data.js`).

[REDACTED]

